Opinion summarization has evolved from extractive methods \cite{erkan2004lexrank,kim2011comprehensive} to neural-based approaches \cite{braz2020_int, amplayo2020unsupervised}, with various specialized directions emerging. For aspect-specific summarization, \citet{angelidis2021extractive} employed VQ-VAE \citep{vandenOord2017}, while \citet{amplayo2021} introduced abstractive approaches using MIL. Self-supervised methods were advanced by \citet{brazinskas2020} using pseudo-summary pairs, enhanced by \citet{amplayo2020} with noisy variations and \citet{elsahar2021} with TF-IDF similarity-based selection. Large-scale processing was addressed by \citet{bhaskar2023} using GPT-3.5 \citep{openai2023} prompting, \citet{jiang-etal-2023-large} with sentiment-aware sampling, and \citet{muddu2024distillingopinionsscaleincremental} through \textsc{XL-OPSUMM}. Multi-source approaches emerged with \cite{Zhao_Chaturvedi_2020} utilizing product descriptions, \cite{Li_Yuan_Xu_Wu_He_Zhou_2020} developing supervised multimodal methods, and \citet{siledar2024b} introducing a structured approach with reviews, descriptions, and Q\&A pairs. While these methods advanced the field, they typically overlook comprehensive product metadata. Our work differs by leveraging LLMs' extended context lengths to incorporate complete product specifications, generating comprehensive summaries that eliminate the need for manual navigation through product information. We extend beyond \citet{siledar2024b} by including detailed specifications and descriptions, providing users with complete product insights in a single, unified summary.
%\textbf{Opinion summarization} has evolved from extractive methods \cite{erkan2004lexrank,kim2011comprehensive} to neural-based approaches \cite{braz2020_int, amplayo2020unsupervised}. These approaches typically aggregate opinions from reviews to provide a consolidated view, but overlook important product features and specifications embedded in the product metadata. As a result, while these summaries capture overall sentiment polarity, they fail to present users with comprehensive information about key product features and attributes that are crucial for making informed purchasing decisions.
%\textbf{Aspect-Specific Opinion Summarization} focuses on identifying and summarizing opinions about specific product features, offering deeper insights into how individuals perceive various aspects of a product. \citet{angelidis2021extractive} pioneered an approach for generating both aspect-specific and general summaries using a Vector Quantized Variational Autoencoder (VQ-VAE) \citep{vandenOord2017} to cluster review sentences. \citet{amplayo2021} introduced the first abstractive approach, identifying aspect-bearing elements through Multiple Instance Learning (MIL). To simplify dataset creation, \citet{shen2023} proposed two methods: SW-LOO, which matches aspect seed words, and NLI-LOO, which uses Natural Language Inference without seed words. \citet{mukherjee2020} took an unsupervised approach, extracting and mapping fine-grained and coarse-grained aspects using Integer Linear Programming (ILP). Recently, \citet{siledar2023a} achieved improved performance by employing off-the-shelf aspect extraction and clustering techniques with cosine similarity and ROUGE scores, removing the need for predefined aspects.
%\textbf{Self-Supervised Opinion Summarization} addresses the challenge of limited labeled data by creating synthetic datasets for training. This approach typically selects one review as a pseudo-summary and uses remaining reviews as input, enabling model training without manual annotations. \citet{brazinskas2020} pioneered this direction by randomly selecting reviews to create pseudo-summary pairs, while \citet{amplayo2020} enhanced it by generating noisy variations of selected reviews. \citet{elsahar2021} improved pair selection using TF-IDF similarity, and \citet{wang-wan-2021-transsum} focused on reducing redundancy through aspect-sentiment embeddings. \citet{im-etal-2021-self} extended this to multimodal data, while \citet{siledar2023a} introduced lexical and semantic similarities for better dataset creation. \citet{siledar2024b} further advanced this by incorporating additional sources like product descriptions and QA pairs. Recent approaches by \citet{bhaskar-etal-2023-prompted} and \citet{hosking-etal-2023-attributable} scale these methods to handle larger review sets.
%\textbf{Large-Scale Opinion Summarization} tackles the challenge of synthesizing opinions from thousands of product reviews on e-commerce platforms. Recent approaches have focused on developing scalable techniques to handle such large volumes of data effectively. \citet{bhaskar2023} explored prompting techniques, particularly with GPT-3.5 \citep{openai2023}, and introduced multiple pipelines for large-scale summarization, while \citet{jiang-etal-2023-large} introduced a sentiment-aware review sampling strategy with a two-stage training framework. \citet{hosking-etal-2023-attributable} proposed encoding reviews into discrete latent spaces and generating summaries from frequent encodings. For incremental processing, \citet{chowdhury2024incrementalextractiveopinionsummarization} developed CoverSumm, a centroid-based approach, while \citet{Chang2023BooookScoreAS} applied incremental and hierarchical approaches to summarizing long-form text, such as books. Finally, \citet{muddu2024distillingopinionsscaleincremental} proposed the XL-OPSUMM framework, which incrementally generates opinion summaries from large collections of review data.
%\textbf{Multi-source Opinion Summarization} involves creating concise summaries that integrate information from diverse sources such as reviews, product descriptions, specifications, and metadata. \cite{Zhao_Chaturvedi_2020} utilized product descriptions for extractive aspect-based summarization, while \cite{Li_Yuan_Xu_Wu_He_Zhou_2020} proposed a supervised multimodal approach incorporating reviews, images, and product details. \citet{im-etal-2021-self} developed a self-supervised multimodal pipeline using reviews, images, and metadata. \citet{siledar2024b} introduced a structured approach generating verdicts, pros, cons, and additional information using reviews, descriptions, and QA pairs through a multi-encoder framework and synthetic pair generation. Our work differs from that of \citet{siledar2024b}, who did not incorporate the complete product metadata when generating opinion summaries. As a result, their approach missed detailed specifications and descriptions of the products. In the era of large language models (LLMs) with extended context lengths, we are, to the best of our knowledge, the first to explore this direction. By utilizing comprehensive product metadata, we generate well-rounded, multi-source opinion summaries, eliminating the need for consumers to navigate product pages and sift through overwhelming amounts of information.
%\textbf{LLM-based Evaluators} Traditional metrics like ROUGE \citep{ro}, BLEU \citep{bleu} and BERTSCORE \cite{zhang-etal-2019-pretraining} correlate poorly with human judgments \cite{shen2023opinsummeval} and inadequately evaluate summaries whereas LLM-based evaluation provides a cost-effective solution to creating large-scale reference-based datasets. LLM-based evaluation methods \citep{fu, chiang-lee-2023-large, closer, wanggpt, kocmi-federmann-2023-large}, including Chain of Thought approaches \citep{liuug, wei2023} and reference-free evaluation \citep{chianggg}. \citet{op} proposed two prompt strategies for opinion summarization. We also leverage large language models (LLMs) as evaluators for summary assessment, employing reference-free evaluation metrics.

\textbf{LLM-based Evaluators} Traditional metrics like ROUGE \citep{ro}, BLEU \citep{bleu} and BERTSCORE \cite{zhang-etal-2019-pretraining} correlate poorly with human judgments \cite{shen2023opinsummeval}. LLM-based evaluation provides a cost-effective solution for large-scale reference-based datasets, including Chain of Thought approaches \citep{liuug, wei2023}, reference-free evaluation \citep{chianggg}, and other methods \citep{fu, chiang-lee-2023-large, closer, wanggpt, kocmi-federmann-2023-large}. \citet{op} proposed two prompt strategies for opinion summarization. We leverage LLMs as evaluators for reference-free \textsc{M-OS} assessment.



\section{Methodology}\label{method}
Our methodology is centered around the development of \textsc{M-OS-PROMPTS}, which facilitate both the \textit{generation} and \textit{evaluation} of M-OS.

\subsection{M-OS-GEN-PROMPT (Summary Generation Prompt)}
The \textsc{M-OS-GEN-PROMPT} guides LLMs to create summaries by synthesizing information from various product attributes, including the product title, description, key features, specifications, customer reviews, and average ratings. By integrating these diverse data sources, the prompt ensures that summaries provide a comprehensive perspective, balancing subjective customer opinions with objective product details. For M-OS-GEN-PROMPT design principles, refer to Appendix ~\ref{gen_design_principle}.

\subsection{M-OS-EVAL-PROMPTS (Summary Evaluation Prompts)}
The \textsc{M-OS-EVAL-PROMPTS} guide evaluation of \textsc{M-OS}, structured to assess $7$ dimensions: \textls[-60]{\textsc{\fl}}, \textls[-60]{\textsc{\co}}, \textls[-30]{\textsc{\rel}}, \textls[-60]{\textsc{\fa}}, \textls[-30]{\textsc{\asp}}, \textls[-30]{\textsc{\sent}}, \textls[-30]{\textsc{\spec}} and \texttt{specificity} (Appendix \ref{metric_definition} for metric definition). Each prompt has four core components for thorough evaluation across open-source and closed-source LLMs:

\noindent(\textit{1}) {\tt \textbf{System Message}}: Defines the LLM's role as a specialized evaluator, providing clear context for the evaluation task.

\noindent(\textit{2}) {\tt \textbf{Task Description}}: Outlines the specific evaluation task, which involves assessing a multi-source opinion summary against product metadata (including title, description, key features, specifications, reviews, and average rating).

\noindent(\textit{3}) {\tt \textbf{Evaluation Criteria}}: Defines the criteria for the task. For multi-source opinion summary evaluation, the LLM assigns a score $(1-5)$ based on how well the summary adheres to a specific metric or dimension.

\noindent(\textit{4}) {\tt \textbf{Evaluation Step}}: Provides the LLM with a detailed, step-by-step guide to complete the evaluation, ensuring consistency and thoroughness.

For M-OS-EVAL-PROMPTS design principles, refer to Appendix \ref{eval_design_principle}. 



\noindent \paragraph{M-OS-EVAL PROMPT Approaches} 
