One of the key issues addressed by cooperative game theory is how to allocate the total profit generated by the grand coalition once it has been formed. This allocation is represented by a vector \( x \in \mathbb{R}^{n} \), where \( n \) denotes the number of players in the set \( N \). The class of superadditive games is particularly notable, as it encourages the formation of the grand coalition to maximize the total profit. Formally, a TU-game \( (N,v) \) is superadditive if for every two coalitions \( S, T \subseteq N \) with \( S \cap T = \emptyset \), it holds that \( v(S \cup T) \geq v(S) + v(T) \). %Furthermore, TU-games where the value of a coalition increases with its size are known as monotonic games. This is equivalent to saying that \( v(T) \geq v(S) \) for all \( S \subseteq T \subseteq N \).


