\begin{abstract}
% Although diffusion models have demonstrated exceptional performance in image generation, they raise critical concerns about copyright issues and model transparency as their adoption grows. Existing attribution methods can only identify which training examples influence an entire generated image. However, in practice, stakeholders often need to understand which examples affect specific elements, such as artistic styles or particular objects. This limitation poses challenges for both legal compliance and model interpretability. To address this, we introduce \emph{concept-level attribution} through a novel approach called \emph{Concept-TRAK}. Our method extends influence functions to trace how training data affects concept-level outputs by integrating a concept-aware reward that emphasizes semantic relevance, thereby correcting poor concept-level attribution in diffusion models. We evaluate our approach using a retrieval-based benchmark, AbC benchmark demonstrating significant improvements over prior attribution methods. Through case studies on detecting IP-protected and unsafe content, and understanding prompt engineering and complex concept learning, we illustrate how concept-level attribution provides valuable insights for addressing practical challenges in generative AI development and governance.
While diffusion models excel at image generation, their growing adoption raises critical concerns around copyright issues and model transparency. Existing attribution methods identify training examples influencing an entire image, but fall short in isolating contributions to specific elements, such as styles or objects, that matter most to stakeholders. To bridge this gap, we introduce \emph{concept-level attribution} via a novel method called \emph{Concept-TRAK}. Concept-TRAK extends influence functions with two key innovations: (1) a reformulated diffusion training loss based on diffusion posterior sampling, enabling robust, sample-specific attribution; and (2) a concept-aware reward function that emphasizes semantic relevance. We evaluate Concept-TRAK on the AbC benchmark, showing substantial improvements over prior methods. Through diverse case studies--ranging from identifying IP-protected and unsafe content to analyzing prompt engineering and compositional learning--we demonstrate how concept-level attribution yields actionable insights for responsible generative AI development and governance.

% Through case studies examining anatomically incorrect hands, prompt misalignments, and social biases, we illustrate how concept-level attribution provides valuable insights for both ethical auditing and understanding how generative models internalize patterns from training data.
% Diffusion models have demonstrated exceptional performance in image generation, raising critical concerns about copyright and data ownership as their use expands. Existing attribution methods can only identify which training examples influence an entire generated image. However, in practice, stakeholders often need to understand influences on specific elements within images—such as artistic styles or particular objects. This gap severely limits both legal compliance efforts and model interpretability. We introduce concept-level attribution, a novel approach that estimates how individual training examples contribute to specific elements within diffusion-generated images.  Our method extends influence functions to concept probabilities and addresses suboptimal \jcc{suboptimal is not a clear word, perhaps need to somehow find a more precise alternative} attribution in diffusion models through a new concept-aware reward formulation. We evaluate our approach using a retrieval-based benchmark across multiple datasets (CIFAR-10, CelebA-HQ, and the AbC benchmark), demonstrating significant improvements over prior attribution methods. Through case studies examining anatomically incorrect hands, prompt misalignments, and social biases, we illustrate how concept-level attribution provides valuable insights for both ethical auditing and understanding how generative models internalize patterns from training data.
\end{abstract}

