\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

Recent years have witnessed remarkable advancements in Large Vision-Language Models (LVLMs) \cite{b72}\cite{b73}, which have demonstrated impressive generalization abilities and surprising zero/few-shot reasoning performance across diverse downstream tasks \cite{b19}\cite{b20}. By leveraging powerful Large Language Models (LLMs) as a brain, LVLMs have outperformed traditional specialized models in a range of multimodal tasks, such as visual question answering \cite{b4,b5}, image captioning \cite{b6,b7}, and image-text retrieval \cite{b8}. However, like LLMs \cite{b9,b10,b11}, LVLMs, which concurrently process both visual and textual modalities, inevitably suffer from hallucination issues, significantly undermining their credibility and reliability.

\begin{figure}[t!]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=0.48\textwidth]{fig_1.pdf}
  \caption{(a) LVLM hallucination, (b)Existing mitigating methods using external tools or comparison of multiple results , (c) Motivation of SEED.}
  \label{fig_1}
\end{figure}

For LVLMs, hallucinations refer to cases where the generated outputs conflict with the visual inputs \cite{b12,b13}. For example, as shown in \cref{fig_1}(a), given an image of a white strawberry, the generated text might incorrectly state that the color of this strawberry is red. One major cause of hallucination is the presence of biases in the internal knowledge of LVLMs \cite{b14,b15}. In the strawberry example, because the model's internal knowledge contains the bias that strawberries are typically red, it tends to ignore the visual information and directly responds that the color of the strawberry is red. To mitigate hallucinations in LVLMs, it is crucial to purge this part of knowledge related to hallucinations.

Existing mainstream methods for mitigating hallucinations in LVLMs can be categorized into three typical types: \textit{i}) The most direct approaches collect larger and more diverse training datasets \cite{b38,b39,b47,b27,b55,b75}, \textit{ii}) Correcting model knowledge through multi-round inference comparisons \cite{b28,b29,b49,b50,b51,b14,b15}, and \textit{iii}) utilizing external knowledge for post-processing \cite{b40,b41,b52,b53,b54}. However, the first type incurs prohibitively high annotation costs, and the latter two types require substantially increased inference costs and latency, as shown in \cref{fig_1}(b). These shortcomings restrict the practicality of these methods to some extent \cite{b28}. Therefore, there is a pressing need in the field for hallucination mitigation methods that are \textit{both low-cost in terms of data annotation and inference}. Compared to collecting new and diverse data, comparing multiple responses, or post-processing, a more promising approach is to prune hallucination-related components within the model's knowledge distribution, thereby addressing hallucination at root.

In this work, we propose \textbf{SE}lf-\textbf{E}volving \textbf{D}istillation (\textbf{\textit{SEED}}), which can  identify and eliminate the hallucination-related components within the model's internal knowledge distribution, as illustrated in \cref{fig_1}(c). SEED can mitigate the hallucination problem of various LVLMs without relying on external models and tools, collecting new data, or increasing inference overhead. Additionally, we design the Mode-Seeking Evolving, which further prevents hallucinations by enabling the model to focus on fitting dominant modes and thereby avoiding hallucination-related void spaces in the output space. Furthermore, we introduce the Hallucination Elimination Adapter to enhance the stability of the distillation process. By isolating the trainable weights, the Hallucination Elimination Adapter stabilizes the source knowledge distribution, thus improving the robustness of the SEED process. The contributions are summarized as follows:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item We propose the self-evolving distillation method, which enables the self-evolution of the LVLM by identifying and  purging hallucinations within its dark knowledge, all without introducing additional data sources, models, or inference overhead.
    \item We propose the Mode-Seeking Evolving method to capture the dominant modes of the purified knowledge distribution while avoiding void spaces in the LVLM output space and the potential chaotic outputs that may result.
    \item We present Hallucination Elimination Adapter, which isolates the trainable parameters during the SEED process, enhancing the stability of knowledge distillation and correcting the dark knowledge of the original model.
    \item Extensive experiments on multiple benchmarks demonstrate the superiority of our method in mitigating hallucinations for representative LVLMs, outperforming existing multi-round methods. Notably, on the POPE-Adversarial hallucination benchmark, the F1 score of LLaVA increases from 81.3 to 85.9, while reducing inference cost by 50\%.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related}
\subsection{Large Vision-Language Models}
Early vision-language models \cite{b7,b56,b57,b58} leveraged the decoder of BERT \cite{b59,b60,b61} to handle the fusion of visual and textual information. With the development of LLMs \cite{b16,b17,b62,b63,b64,b65}, vision-language models have gradually evolved into LVLMs \cite{b18,b19,b20,b66,b67}. The training of LVLMs, typically based on pre-trained LLMs, generally involves two stages \cite{b19,b42}: the first is a pre-training phase focused on modality alignment, and the second is an instruction fine-tuning phase aimed at enhancing task-solving capabilities \cite{b5}. Compared to earlier visual-language models, large visual-language models exhibit superior task performance and generalization ability \cite{b4,b6,b7,b8}.

\subsection{Hallucinations in LVLMs}
The hallucination problem in LVLMs refers to conflicts between the generated responses and the visual input \cite{b23,b24,b25}. For example, the model may mention objects that are not present in the image or provide incorrect descriptions of an object’s quantity or attributes. Existing mitigation strategies typically involve collecting larger and more diverse datasets \cite{b26,b27,b38,b39,b47,b27,b55}, which incurs significant costs, or relying on multi-step reasoning \cite{b28,b29,b49,b50,b51,b14} or post-processing \cite{b40,b41,b52,b53,b54} techniques such as external knowledge retrieval to refine the results. However, these approaches introduce substantial overhead in terms of data collection or inference cost.

\begin{figure}[t!]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=0.47\textwidth]{corr.png}
  \caption{The correlation between confidence and answer scores of LLaVA1.5-7B on LLaVA-Bench-in-the-Wild. Confidence is positively correlated with scores: the higher the confidence, the higher the score, and the lower the probability of hallucination.}
  \label{corr}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Knowledge Distillation}
KD \cite{b30,b31} aims to transfer knowledge to a smaller model to reduce inference costs while maintaining performance. For LLMs, the standard KD method typically involves minimizing of the forward KLD between the student and teacher's generative distributions \cite{b68}, or directly training on the text generated by the teacher \cite{b69,b70,b71}. However, due to the large output distribution space, the commonly used forward KL loss can lead to the student LLM assigning high probabilities to invalid regions \cite{b33}. As a result, several approaches \cite{b46,b34,b35} have proposed using alternative distillation losses, such as Jensen-Shannon divergence, in LLMs. However, the selection of loss functions for distillation LVLMs still requires further exploration.

\begin{figure*}[t!]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{main_fig.pdf}
  \caption{\textbf{Pipeline of Our Method.} First, we use a hallucination probing dataset to extract the internal knowledge of the model. Then, in hallucination identification, we evaluate the likelihood of each piece of knowledge containing hallucinations based on confidence scores and determine the required purification degree accordingly. In hallucination isolation, we introduce perturbations to the visual information to amplify hallucinations, thereby obtaining a isolated hallucination distribution. In hallucination purification, we remove the hallucination distribution from the knowledge distribution according to the purification degree. Finally, we distill the purified knowledge into the hallucination elimination adapter using mode-seeking evolving to refine the internal knowledge distribution of the LVLM.}
  \label{main_fig}
\end{figure*}

\section{Self-Evolving Distillation}
\label{method}

\subsection{Hallucination Identification}
To purge hallucinations within the internal knowledge of LVLMs, we first identify them using a small-scale hallucination probing dataset. For each pair of image and question in this dataset, we feed it into the LVLM to obtain $Logits_o$, which contains the model's dark knowledge:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}ogits_o = LVLM(\mathcal{I}mage,\mathcal{Q}uestion)
\label{eq1}
\end{equation}
To identify whether hallucinations exist in each piece of dark knowledge, we propose a confidence-based identification method. When LVLM shows low confidence in its own output, it indicates that the knowledge is near or even beyond its capability boundary, making hallucinations more likely \cite{b77}. Therefore, we convert the hallucination identification problem into assessing confidence.

$Logits_o$ represents the output distribution of this latent knowledge; after applying the Softmax function to this distribution, a sequence of probabilities can be obtained.
\begin{equation}
\{\mathcal{P}rob_0,..., \mathcal{P}rob_{N-1}\}=Softmax(\mathcal{L}ogits_o)
\label{eq1_1}
\end{equation}
where $N$ represents sequence length.

The higher the overall output probability of an answer sequence, the higher its confidence. The output probability of an answer sequence can be obtained by multiplying the maximum probability values at each position. However, since multiplying several very small probability values may exceed precision limits, we choose the logarithm of the probability as the evaluation metric for confidence.
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{C}onf=log(\prod_{i=0}^{N-1}\mathcal{MP}rob_i)= \sum_{i=0}^{N-1} log(\mathcal{MP}rob_i) 
\label{eq1_2}
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{C}onf$ represents the estimated confidence, $\mathcal{MP}rob_i$ denotes the max probability within $\mathcal{P}rob_i$.

In this way, we obtain an estimate of the confidence in a piece of dark knowledge, which can serve as an indicator of hallucination. When the confidence is low, we consider it likely that this dark knowledge contains hallucination, which can be supported by \cref{corr}.

\subsection{Hallucination Isolation}
After identifying the presence of hallucination in a piece of dark knowledge, the next step is to isolate the hallucinated part of knowledge.
\begin{figure*}[t!]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{case_study.pdf}
  \caption{Two examples where hallucinations in LLaVA-1.5 have been mitigated after SEED.}
  \label{case_study}
\end{figure*}

The hallucinations in an LVLM primarily reside within the LLM component \cite{b9}. During the pre-training of the LLM on the text modality, it learns many statistical priors. For example, strawberries are generally red and bananas are generally yellow. During inference, the LVLM might over-rely on the text modality and the biases within the LLM, neglecting visual information. For instance, when asked about the color of a strawberry, the model might respond ``red" based on the textual input, even if the provided image depicts a white strawberry \cite{b15}.

To isolate these hallucinations, we aim to weaken the influence of visual information, resulting in an answer predominantly driven by  text modality. In this way, the LVLM will inevitably rely on the biases it has learned from the text modality. Therefore, we first add noise to the visual input:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{I}mage' = \sqrt{\alpha} \, \mathcal{I}mage + \sqrt{1 - \alpha} \, \epsilon,
\label{eq2}
\end{equation}
where \(\epsilon \sim \mathcal{N}(0, I)\), \(\alpha\) is the noise strength, and $\mathcal{I}mage'$ represents the image with noise. In this way, we reduce the amount of visual information received by the LVLM, leading to an answer dominated by text-based information and statistical biases:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}ogits_h = LVLM(\mathcal{I}mage',\mathcal{Q}uestion)
\label{eq3}
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{L}ogits_h$ contains isolated hallucinations. 

\subsection{Hallucination Purification}
To eliminate hallucinations within $Logits_o$, we perform a weighted subtraction between these two logits to obtain purified logits:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}ogits_p = (1+\mathcal{\beta}) \mathcal{L}ogits_o - \beta \mathcal{L}ogits_h
\label{eq4}
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{L}ogits_p$ represents purified Logits, $\beta$ indicates the purification degree. The larger the value of $\beta$, the stronger the suppression of hallucinations. $\beta$ is determined by estimated confidence $\mathcal{C}onf$.
\begin{equation}
\beta = \beta_i,\ if \ \ \mathcal{QT}_i<\mathcal{C}onf<\mathcal{QT}{i+1},0<=i<G
\label{eq4_1}
\end{equation}
where \( G \) is the number of groups, \( \mathcal{QT}_i \) is the \(i/G\) quantile of the Conf distribution, \( split_0 \) is \(-\infty\), and \( split_G \) is \( \infty \). The Conf distribution is obtained from the statistics of a subset of the hallucination probing dataset. \( \beta_0 \) to \( \beta_{G-1} \) are predefined constants in descending order.

\subsection{Distilling Knowledge Back}
Then, we leverage the purified knowledge to adjust the distribution of the original LVLM, purging its internal hallucinations. Knowledge distillation is a technique that allows a student model to mimic the behavior of a teacher model or system. In this case, we can treat the purified knowledge as the teacher and the original LVLM as the student, removing hallucinations from the internal knowledge of the LVLM. The loss function for the distillation process is as follows:
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\mathcal{L} = 
&\ \lambda \, \mathcal{L}_\mathcal{D} \left( 
Softmax(\mathcal{L}ogits_d) 
\parallel 
Softmax(\mathcal{L}ogits_p) 
\right) \\
& + (1 - \lambda) \, \mathcal{L}_{CE} \left( 
Softmax(\mathcal{L}ogits_d), 
y_t 
\right)
\label{eq5}
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{L}_\mathcal{D}$ is the distillation loss, $\mathcal{L}ogits_d$ is the logits to be distilled, $\mathcal{L}_{CE}$ is the cross-entropy loss, $y_t$ denotes the ground truth, and $\lambda$ is a constant.

After updating the LVLM based on the gradients of this loss, the model’s hallucinations are reduced.
\begin{equation}
\theta_{M{new}} = \theta_{M{old}} - \eta \nabla_{\theta_M} \mathcal{L}
\label{eq6}
\end{equation}
where $\theta_M$ represents parameters of LVLM. By distilling the purified knowledge back into the original model, the LVLM achieves  hallucination suppression without adding additional inference overhead.

\section{Mode-Seeking Evolving}

In Self-Evolving Distillation, the loss function is crucial as it determines how effectively the distilled LVLM mimics the Logits of purified knowledge. In white-box knowledge distillation, the KL divergence is the most commonly used loss function. KL divergence measures the distance between distribution \( Q \) and distribution \( P \):
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{D}_{KL}(\mathcal{P} \parallel \mathcal{Q}) = \mathbb{E}_{x \sim \mathcal{P}} \left[ \log \frac{\mathcal{P}(X)}{\mathcal{Q}(X)} \right]
\label{eq9}
\end{equation}

KL divergence is asymmetric, meaning \( \mathcal{D}_{\text{KL}}(\mathcal{P} \parallel \mathcal{Q}) \) is not equal to \( \mathcal{D}_{\text{KL}}(\mathcal{Q} \parallel \mathcal{P}) \). In terms of Self-Evolving Distillation, the forward KL divergence can be written as:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{D}_{KL}(\mathcal{K} \parallel \mathcal{D}_\theta) = \mathbb{E}_{x \sim \mathcal{K}} \left[ \log \frac{\mathcal{K}(X)}{\mathcal{D}_\theta(X)} \right]
\label{eq10}
\end{equation}
where \( \mathcal{K} \) represents the distribution of the purified knowledge, and \( \mathcal{D}_\theta \) represents the distilled distribution.

By transforming \cref{eq10}, the optimization objective can be equivalently expressed as:
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
&\arg \min_{\theta} \mathcal{D}_{KL}(\mathcal{K} \parallel \mathcal{D}_\theta)\\
&=\arg \min_{\theta} \mathbb{E}_{x \sim \mathcal{K}} \left[ - \log \mathcal{D}_\theta(X)\right] - \mathcal{H}(\mathcal{K}(X)) \\
&= \arg \min_{\theta} \mathbb{E}_{x \sim \mathcal{K}} \left[ - \log \mathcal{D}_\theta(X) \right] \\
&= \arg \max_{\theta} \mathbb{E}_{x \sim \mathcal{K}} \left[ \log \mathcal{D}_\theta(X) \right]\\
\end{aligned}
\label{eq11}
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{H}(\mathcal{K}(X))$ is the entropy of the distribution $\mathcal{K}$.

We can find that the objective of the forward KL divergence is forcing \( \mathcal{D}_\theta \) to assign high probability where \( \mathcal{K} \) has high probability. This leads to \textbf{mean-seeking behavior}, meaning that the distilled model's distribution \( \mathcal{D}_\theta \) tries to cover all high-probability regions (modes) of \( \mathcal{K} \), as shown in \cref{fig_kl}. As a result, \( \mathcal{D}_\theta \)'s distribution center tends to lie in intermediate regions between modes of \( \mathcal{K} \) to cover as many modes as possible simultaneously. The forward KL divergence does not penalize \( \mathcal{D}_\theta \) for assigning high probability to \( \mathcal{K} \)'s low-probability regions.

This mean-seeking behavior can degrade effectiveness of SEED. Because the output space of large models is vast and \( \mathcal{K} \) contains numerous modes, mean-seeking behavior causes \( \mathcal{D}_\theta \) to lie in intermediate regions among multiple modes of \( \mathcal{K} \). These intermediate regions are typically void spaces in the space of large models \cite{b32}. Assigning high probability to void spaces causes the distilled model to produce chaotic results during inference.

In Self-Evolving Distillation, the reverse KL divergence is expressed as:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{D}_{KL}(\mathcal{D}_\theta \parallel \mathcal{K}) = \mathbb{E}_{x \sim \mathcal{D}_\theta(X)} \left[ \log \frac{\mathcal{D}_\theta (X)}{\mathcal{K}(X)} \right]
\label{eq12}
\end{equation}

Transforming the expression of the reverse KL divergence, the optimization objective becomes:
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
&\arg\min_{\theta} \mathcal{D}_{KL}(\mathcal{D}_\theta \parallel \mathcal{K}) \\
&= \arg\min_{\theta} \mathbb{E}_{x \sim \mathcal{D}_\theta} \left[ -\log \mathcal{K}(X)\right] - \mathcal{H}(\mathcal{D}_\theta(X)) \\
&= \arg\max_{\theta} \mathbb{E}_{x \sim \mathcal{D}_\theta} \left[ \log \mathcal{K}(X)\right] + \mathcal{H}(\mathcal{D}_\theta (X))\\
\end{aligned}
\label{eq13}
\end{equation}

–\begin{figure}[t!]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=0.48\textwidth]{distribution.png}
  \caption{Toy experiment fitting Gaussian Mixture Distribution using Reverse KLD and Forward KLD. (Reverse KLD can fit dominant modes better and avoid the target distribution's low-probability regions.)}
  \label{fig_kl}
