From the equation above, we understand that the reverse KL divergence requires that \( \mathcal{K} \) assigns high probability where \( \mathcal{D}_\theta \) has high probability. The reverse KL divergence leads to \textbf{mode-seeking behavior}: since \( \mathcal{D}_\theta \) must focus on regions where \( \mathcal{K} \) is high, it will concentrate on the dominant modes of \( \mathcal{K} \). In other words, \( \mathcal{D}_\theta \) will attempt to fit dominant modes of purified knowledge distribution \( \mathcal{K} \), avoiding void spaces. This can improve the effectiveness of SEED significantly.

Additionally, conventional knowledge distillation is performed on the ground truth token sequences provided by the dataset, with the information solely derived from the logits. In SEED, to better distill the purified knowledge into the model, we propose performing distillation on sequences sampled from the purified knowledge distribution. In SEED, the loss is computed as follows:
\begin{equation*}
\mathcal{L}_{\mathcal{D}} = \mathcal{D}_{KL}\left(Softmax(\mathcal{L}ogits_d ) \parallel Softmax( \mathcal{L}ogits_p ) \right) 
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation}
\left\{
\begin{aligned}
\mathcal{L}_{\mathcal{S}} &= \mathcal{L}_{CE} \left(Softmax(\mathcal{L}ogits_d ), y_s \right),P= \gamma \\
\mathcal{L}_{\mathcal{S}} &= \mathcal{L}_{CE} \left(Softmax(\mathcal{L}ogits_d ), y_t \right),P= 1-\gamma
\end{aligned}
\right.
\end{equation}
\begin{equation*}
\mathcal{L} = \mathcal{L}_{\mathcal{D}} + \mathcal{L}_{\mathcal{S}}
\end{equation*}
where $y_s$ represents sampled sequence from purified knowledge distribution, $\gamma$ is the probability of distillation applied to the sampled token sequence, and $\mathcal{L}_{\mathcal{D}}$ and $\mathcal{L}_{\mathcal{S}}$ represent the distillation loss and supervision loss, respectively.

\section{Hallucination Elimination Adapter}
SEED extracts knowledge from an LVLM, purifies it, and then distills it back into the same LVLM, enabling the self-evolution of the LVLM. During this process, the LVLM is continuously updated. Using a continuously updating LVLM as the knowledge source can lead to unstable knowledge extraction. The unstable knowledge is then used for further updates to the LVLM, creating a feedback loop that causes instability in the SEED process. To ensure the stability of the SEED process, the stability of the knowledge source must be guaranteed. However, creating a frozen separate copy of the original LVLM as the knowledge source leads to a significant increase in VRAM consumption during the distillation process.

\begin{algorithm}[t]
\caption{Self-Evolving Distillation}
\label{alg:SEVPD}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\STATE \textbf{Input:} $\text{LVLM}$, $\alpha$,  $\lambda$, $\gamma$, Hallucination Elimination Adapter $A_{he}$
\STATE Initialize \text{Hallucination Elimination Adapter} $A_{he}$.
\FOR{each distillation iteration}
\STATE Get $\mathcal{I}mage$, $\mathcal{Q}uestion$, Ground Truth sequence $y_t$.
    \STATE \textbf{Generate Purified Logits:}
        \STATE $\mathcal{L}ogits_o = LVLM(\mathcal{I}mage, \mathcal{Q}uestion)$;
        \STATE Estimate $\mathcal{C}onf$ using \cref{eq1_2};
        \STATE $\mathcal{I}mage' = \sqrt{\alpha} \, \mathcal{I}mage + \sqrt{1 - \alpha} \, \epsilon$;
        \STATE $\mathcal{L}ogits_h = LVLM(\mathcal{I}mage', \mathcal{Q}uestion)$;
        \STATE Select $\beta$ according to $\mathcal{C}onf$ using \cref{eq4_1};
        \STATE $\mathcal{L}ogits_p = (1+\beta)*\mathcal{L}ogits_o - \beta * \mathcal{L}ogits_h$;  \STATE Sample $y_s$ from Purified Distribution.

    \STATE \textbf{Self-Evloving Distillation:}

    \STATE $\mathcal{L}ogits_d = LVLM(\mathcal{I}mage, \mathcal{Q}uestion, A_{he})$;    

    \STATE $\mathcal{L}_{\mathcal{D}} = \mathcal{D}_{KL}\left(\mathcal{L}ogits_d \parallel \mathcal{L}ogits_p \right)$;

    \STATE $\mathcal{L}_{\mathcal{S}} = \mathcal{L}_{CE} \left(\mathcal{L}ogits_d , y_s \right), P = \gamma$;

    \STATE $\mathcal{L}_{\mathcal{S}} = \mathcal{L}_{CE} \left(\mathcal{L}ogits_d, y_t \right), P = 1- \gamma$;

    \STATE $\mathcal{L} = \mathcal{L}_{\mathcal{D}} + \mathcal{L}_{\mathcal{S}}$;

    \STATE Update $A_{he}$ parameters with gradient of $\mathcal{L}$
\ENDFOR

\STATE \textbf{Output:} Distilled $A_{he}$, when inserted, can purify LVLM's knowledge and mitigate hallucinations.
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

Therefore, we propose the Hallucination Elimination Adapter, which freezes the original LVLM as the knowledge source while only updating the inserted Adapter during the SEED process. This approach not only reduces VRAM consumption but also enhances the stability of the SEED process. During the SEED process using the Hallucination Elimination Adapter, $Logits_o, Logits_h \text{ and } Logits_d$ are obtained as follows:
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
& \mathcal{L}ogits_o = LVLM_{Frozen}(\mathcal{I}mage,\mathcal{Q}uestion) \\
& \mathcal{L}ogits_h = LVLM_{Frozen}(\mathcal{I}mage',\mathcal{Q}uestion) \\
& \mathcal{L}ogits_d = LVLM_{+A_{he}}(\mathcal{I}mage,\mathcal{Q}uestion) 
\label{eq7}
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
where $A_{he}$ represents Hallucination Elimination Adapter. The update process of Hallucination Elimination Adapter is as follows:
\begin{equation}
\theta_{A_{he}new} = \theta_{A_{he}old} - \eta \nabla_{\theta_{A_{he}}} \mathcal{L}
\label{eq8}
\end{equation}

Hallucination Elimination Adapter isolates the trainable weights during the SEED process, ensuring the stability and consistency of the knowledge source, which enhances the stability of SEED.

