\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}


\subsection{Experimental Settings}
\noindent{\textbf{Baseline Models.}} We selected two representative open-source LVLMs as baselines: LLaVA-1.5 \cite{b19} and InternVL2 \cite{b42}. LLaVA-1.5 is the most widely used baseline, while InternVL2 offers the most complete range of model scales, allowing us to validate the generalization of our method on larger-scale models. In addition to these original models, we also compared against two advanced LVLM hallucination mitigation methods: Visual Contrast Decoding \cite{b15}, which doubles the inference time, and Caption Rewriting \cite{b75}, which rewrites the training data.


\noindent{\textbf{Implementation Details.}} For our SEED experiments, we chose the LLaVA 665k instruction tuning dataset as the hallucination probing dataset, and randomly select 1\% data in it to estimate the distribution of confidence. In the hyperparameter configuration, we set $\alpha$ to 0.3,  $\gamma$ to 0.1, $G$ to 4 and $\lambda$ to 0.1. For LLaVA, $\beta_0$ to $\beta_3$ are set to 1.1, 0.8, 0.6, 0.4. For InternVL2, they are set to 0.5, 0.2, 0.1, 0.05. During SEED, the Hallucination Elimination Adapter utilizes a rank-128 LoRA \cite{b74} with alpha set to 256. The learning rates for LLaVA and InternVL are set to 2e-4 and 4e-5, respectively. The SEED process is conducted for one epoch.

