\subsection{Evaluation Benchmarks}
In our experiment, we selected three widely used benchmarks for assessing LVLM hallucinations: POPE \cite{b43}, MME \cite{b45} and MM-Vet \cite{b76}.

\begin{figure*}[t!]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=0.95\textwidth]{mme.pdf}
  \caption{Comparison of different hallucination mitigation methods on MME with LLaVA-1.5-7B \cite{b19}.}
  \label{mme}
\end{figure*}


\textbf{POPE} (Polling-based Object Probing Evaluation) \cite{b43} is a method used to assess object hallucinations in LVLMs. In POPE, the model is required to determine whether a specific object is present in a given image. The MSCOCO \cite{b44} dataset is used as the source of test data, and POPE constructs query data with three different settings: \textit{Random setting}, where negative objects are randomly selected; \textit{Popular setting}, where negative objects come from the most frequent objects in the dataset; and \textit{Adversarial setting}, where negative objects are highly related to existing objects in the image but do not actually appear in it. The evaluation results are based on multiple metrics, including accuracy, precision, recall, and F1 score, providing a comprehensive assessment of the model's handling of object hallucinations.

\textbf{MME} \cite{b45} aims to provide a comprehensive evaluation of LVLMs on both perceptual and cognitive abilities. The perceptual evaluation focuses on basic information recognition, such as the existence, count, position, and color, while the cognitive evaluation includes tasks such as commonsense reasoning, numerical calculations, text translation, and code reasoning. By employing simple ``yes" or ``no" instructions, MME is able to quantitatively analyze the model outputs, ensuring the accuracy and objectivity.

\textbf{MM-Vet} \cite{b76} is a comprehensive benchmark used to assess six core capabilities of LVLMs. Compared to the single-word responses of POPE and MME, MM-Vet provides natural long-text answers, with the response quality evaluated by GPT-4.

