\subsection{Experimental Results on POPE and MME}

In \cref{table_main} , we present the POPE metrics of InternVL and LLaVA after SEED, and compare them with the results from Visual Contrast Decoding (VCD) \cite{b15}. In \cref{mme}, we visualize MME metrics for the original LLaVA-1.5-7B \cite{b19}, the version utilizing VCD, and the one enhanced with SEED. As can be seen, after applying our proposed SEED, the LVLM shows improvements across all metrics on both POPE and MME, with most of the metrics surpassing those achieved by VCD, which requires twice the inference cost.

\subsection{Experimental Results on MM-Vet}

In \cref{table4}, We present the MM-Vet scores of LLaVA and InternVL after utilizing different hallucination mitigation methods. The results show that our SEED consistently achieves the best hallucination mitigation performance.

\subsection{Ablation Studies}

We conducted three types of ablation experiments to explore the effectiveness of various configurations: ablation of the distillation type, ablation of the Hallucination Identification, and ablation of the Hallucination Elimination Adapter.

In the distillation type ablation, we compared forward KLD, forward KLD + reverse KLD, and JSD. The results are shown in \cref{table2}. Across all experiments, Model-Seeking Evolving yields the best performance.

Additionally, we performed an ablation experiment on the Hallucination Elimination Adapter and Hallucination Identification. As shown in \cref{table3}, the training results without the  Hallucination Elimination Adapter or Hallucination Identification were significantly worse. This demonstrates that Hallucination Adapter and Hallucination Identification play roles in stabilizing training and accurately identifying hallucinations, respectively.

\subsection{Case study}
In \cref{case_study}, we present two examples where hallucinations are suppressed after SEED. The original LLaVA exhibited biases, such as associating Cristiano Ronaldo solely with soccer ball and bananas exclusively with yellow. As a result, it overlooked the ping pong ball and the red banana in the image, responding based on its biases. After applying SEED, the model's responses align with the image input, achieving consistency, which demonstrates the value of our SEED in practical applications.

