 Recall the definition of $\Bdt$ as a realization of the set $ \Bdet$ and the definition of $\Bdc$ as a realization of the set $\Bdec$. To decode the eMBB message, we again have the two error events defined in \eqref{eq:Ee1} and \eqref{eq:Ee2}. The average eMBB decoding error probability is bounded as
\begin{IEEEeqnarray}{rCl} \label{eq:61}
\epsilon_{\e, \text{SIC}}^{(n)} &\le&
\sum_{B_{\text{dt}}} \Pr [\Bdet = B_{\text{dt}}] \Big ( \Pr [\mathcal E_{\e,1}| \Bdet = B_{\text{dt}}] \notag \\
&&+\sum_{\Bdc} \Pr[\Bdec = \Bdc|\Bdet = \Barr, \Bdet = \Bdt]  \notag \\
&& \hspace{0.5cm}\Pr [\mathcal E_{\e,2} | \Bdet = \Bdt , \Bdec = \Bdc] \Big). \label{eq:71}
\end{IEEEeqnarray}
Note that, compared to the TIN approach, here the decoding of the eMBB message not only depends on the detected URLLC messages set $\Bdt$ but also depends on the correctly decoded URLLC messages set $\Bdc \subset \Bdt$. 
