 \begin{abstract}
In this paper, we consider a point-to-point integrated sensing and communication (ISAC) system. In this system, a transmitter wishes to convey a message to a receiver over a channel with memory and simultaneously estimate the state of the channel through the backscattered signals from the emitted waveform. Using Massey’s concept of directed information for channels with memory, we formulate the capacity-distortion tradeoff for the ISAC problem. Optimizing the transmit waveform for this system to simultaneously achieve good communication  and  sensing performance is a complicated task, and thus we propose a deep reinforcement learning (RL) approach to find a solution. The proposed RL approach enables the agent to optimize the ISAC performance by learning a reward that reflects the difference between the communication gain and the sensing loss. We use the deep deterministic policy gradient  algorithm (DDPG) to model the RL problem, and modify it by incorporating memory to the action space. Our numerical results suggest a significant performance improvement of channels with memory over the memoryless cases by achieving higher communication gain while satisfying the distortion threshold. The results thus emphasize the necessity of well exploiting the memory that is inherent in such systems. 
\end{abstract}
\section{Introduction}
Integrated sensing and communication (ISAC) in a system can be enabled by several features anticipated for 6G communication systems: higher frequency bands (from mmWave up to THz), wider bandwidths and denser distributions of massive antenna arrays. Such integration can not only enhance the mutual performance of both sensing and communication but it can also reduce the overall cost, size, and power consumption of the network \cite{Visa2024,Luo2024, Liu2022, Ahmadi2024, Calderbank2023}. 

Recently, deep learning (DL) technology has demonstrated its powerful  capability in various wireless communication applications such as channel estimation, signal detection, and resource allocation \cite{Poorbook}. Motivated by this, some recent studies have focused on enhancing the joint sensing and communication performance of an ISAC system using deep reinforcement learning approaches in different model-based and model-free settings \cite{Pulkkinen2024,PulkkinenICASSP,Nguyen2024} and in a wide range of applications from vehicular communications \cite{CLiu2022} to unmanned aerial vehicles \cite{Lin2023}.

 In this paper, we propose an RL approach to ISAC for channels with memory by adopting a deep deterministic policy gradient (DDPG) algorithm \cite{Lillicrap2015} where an agent simultaneously takes sensing and communication actions to optimize ISAC performance. More specifically, we first use Massey’s concept of directed information for channels with memory \cite{Massey1990} and formulate the capacity-distortion trade-off of a class of channels with memory known as unifilar finite state channels (FSC)s \cite{Gallager1968}. This formulation presents an optimization problem where the objective is to optimize to transmit waveform to simultaneously achieve good communication and sensing  performance. To solve this optimization problem,  we first provide a Markov decision process (MDP) formulation of the  problem. We then use a DDPG algorithm to numerically evaluate the capacity-distortion trade-off. 
 %To numerically evaluate this approach, we consider a binary multiplicative state channel with memory and use a novel change of state method to transform it to a unifilar FSC. 
 Our numerical results suggest a significant performance improvement of channels with memory over memoryless cases by achieving higher communication gains while satisfying a distortion threshold. Note that the information-theoretic analysis of capacity-distortion trade-off of  ISAC systems in both asymptotic \cite{Kobayashi2019,Ahmadipour2022} and finite blocklength \cite{Nikbakht2024} regimes have been studied in the literature but only for memoryless channels. The work in \cite{Chen2023}  studies the ISAC problem with a general action-dependent state and channel setting, with different noisy side information available at the encoder and decoder sides. 

