To introduce different levels of memory, let $1<k<n$ be an integer.  Given $k$, at each time $i$, we limit the search for the last bit $1$ that has been sent over the channel to the previous $k$ codewords, i.e., $1 \le \ell_i \le \max \{i-1, i-k\}$. More specifically, at each time ~$i$, to search for the last bit~$1$ that has been sent over the channel,  we only look at the previous codewords $X_{i-1}, X_{i-2}, \ldots, X_{\max \{1, i-k\}}$.   
Fig.~\ref{fig2} illustrates the average reward versus the parameter $\beta$ for  $n = 5000$ and $p = 0.1$. The plots are for the infinite memory (i.e., $k = n$), $k = 500$, $k = 2000$ and  memoryless cases.  In the memoryless case, the agent is trained regardless of the fact  that the channel has memory.  In the implementation, we have trained the agent for $500$ episodes each containing $100$ consecutive blocks. The Monte Carlo evaluation  length for the average reward is equal to $1000$. Reward values for setups with memory and without memory were significantly different for all $\beta$ values ($P_{\text{value}}<0.001$, Wilcoxon test, corrected for multiple comparisons by false discovery rate (FDR) for dependent samples with an $\alpha$ level of 0.05).  As can be seen from this figure, in the case with memory, the average reward is higher than in the case without  memory. The gap increases as we increase the level of memory $k$. %effect of increasing the parameter $\beta$ is decreased. 
Fig.~\ref{fig3} illustrates the communication gain versus $\D$ for the with and without memory cases for $p = 0.3$.  As can be seen from this figure, the case with memory significantly outperforms the memoryless case.  Fig.~\ref{fig5} illustrates the average reward as a function of $\beta$ and $p$ for  the infinite memory, (i.e., $k = 5000$), $k = 1000$, $k = 2000$ and  the memoryless cases. As can be seen from this figure, as $\beta$ increases, the average reward decreases in all cases. Regarding the parameter $p$, the average reward has a higher value for very large ( i.e., $p> 0.8$) and very small (i.e., $p<0.2$) values of $p$. For all the values of $p$ except $p = 0.5$, the average reward increases with the increase of the memory level $k$. For $p = 0.5$, the average reward is the same for the memoryless and  with memory cases. This is due to the fact that when $p = 0.5$, then for all values of $i$,  $\pli = 0.5$ and  channels are equivalents regardless of their level of memory. 
\section{Conclusions}
We have considered an ISAC system where  a transmitter sends a message to a receiver over a channel with memory and simultaneously estimates the state of the channel through the backscattered signals from the emitted waveform. We have used Massey’s concept of directed information for channels with memory and formulated the capacity-distortion tradeoff for the unifilar finite state channels. We then presented an MDP formulation of the resulting waveform optimization problem and solved it by employing the DDPG algorithm.  Our numerical results have shown a significant performance improvement of channels with memory over the memoryless cases by achieving higher communication gain while satisfying the distortion threshold. 
