\section{Proof of Lemma~\ref{lem1}} \label{App:A}
 From \eqref{eq:pli}, $\pli$ is given by 
 \begin{IEEEeqnarray}{rCl}
 \pli
 &=&  \Pr [\tilde S_{i-\ell_i+1} \oplus \tilde S_{i-\ell_i + 2} \oplus \ldots \oplus \tilde S_i  = 1] \\
 & = & \Pr [\tilde S_{i-\ell_i+1} = 0] \Pr [\tilde S_{i-\ell_i+2}  \oplus \ldots \oplus \tilde S_i  = 1] \notag \\
 &&+   \Pr [\tilde S_{i-\ell_i+1} = 1] \Pr [\tilde S_{i-\ell_i+2}  \oplus \ldots \oplus \tilde S_i  = 0] \IEEEeqnarraynumspace \\
% & = & (1-p) p_{\ell_i -1} + p (1-p_{\ell_i-1}) \\
 & = & (1-2p) p_{\ell_i -1}  + p. \label{eq:pir}
 \end{IEEEeqnarray} 
Note that \eqref{eq:pir} is equivalent to a first-order non-homogeneous linear recurrence of the form $a_n = \alpha a_{n-1} + \beta$ with $\alpha = (1-2p)$ and $\beta = p$. Thus we can use the general method of determining the closed formula for $a_n$. To this end, we define $\eta = \beta/(1-\alpha)$ and then we have 
\begin{equation}
a_n - \eta = \alpha(a_{n-1} -\eta).
\end{equation}
which is of  a first-order homogeneous recurrence form. Thus
\begin{equation}
a_n -\eta = (a_0-\eta) \alpha^n.
\end{equation}
By replacing $\alpha$,  $\beta$, $\eta$ and $a_0$ with $(1-2p)$, $p$, $\frac{1}{2}$ and $0$ respectively, we have
\begin{equation} 
a_n = \frac{1}{2} - \frac{1}{2} (1-2p)^n.
\end{equation}
This proves the lemma.

