\paragraph{Gender} 
Among LLM learners, 53\% participants were male ($n=358$) and 46\% ($n=313$) female (see \autoref{fig:gender}), whereas in the group that avoided using LLMs for learning, women formed a majority with 64\% ($n=53$ female, $n=25$ male). A chi-square test found this difference significant ($\tilde{\chi}^2=15.5$, $p<0.001$), meaning that men in our participants' sample were more likely to embrace LLMs as learning tools than women.


\paragraph{Education Level}
Educational attainment was relatively high overall, but non-LLM users had lower education levels on average (albeit not siginificant). 40\% of non-users ($n=6$) hold at least a bachelor's degree, compared to 57–61\% ($n=386$ and $n=51$) of those who use LLMs (with or without learning, respectively). 

