	\begin{claim}\label{3-dist}
		$\deg_{T}(t_4) = 2$.
	\end{claim}
	\begin{proof}[Proof of \cref{3-dist}]
		Suppose, to the contrary, that $\deg_{T}(t_4)= 3$, implying that triangle $F_4$ is an internal triangle of $G$. We have shown the shaded triangle $F_4$ of $G$ corresponding to vertex $t_4$ in Figure~\ref{triangle}. In the following, we consider two cases depending on whether $V(F_4)=\{u_2,u_5,u_6\}$ or $V(F_4)=\{u_4,u_5,u_6\}$.

		Suppose firstly that $V(F_4)=\{u_2,u_5,u_6\}$. Let $G' = G - \{u_1,u_3,u_4\}$ be a graph of order $n'$ obtained by deleting the vertices $u_1$, $u_3$ and $u_4$. The resulting graph $G'$ is a mop of order $n' = n - 3 \ge 10$ with $k-1$ number of vertices of degree~$2$ since $F_4$ is an internal triangle of $G$. The edge $u_2u_5$ is an outer edge of $G'$. Let $G_1$ be graph of order $n_1$ obtained from $G'$ by contracting the edge $u_2u_5$ to form a new vertex $x$ in $G_1$, and let $G_1$ have $k_1$ vertices of degree~$2$. By Lemma~\ref{key}, $G_1$ is a mop. Since $n' \ge 10$, we note that $n_1 = n' - 1 \ge 9$. By the minimality of the mop $G$, we have $\gamma_2^d(G_1) \le \floor*{\frac{2}{9}(n_1+k_1)} \le \floor*{\frac{2}{9}(n-4+k-1)} \le \floor*{\frac{2}{9}(n+k)}-1$. Let $D_1$ be a $\gamma_2^d$-set of $G_1$. If $x \in D_1$, then let $D = (D_1\setminus \{x\}) \cup \{u_2,u_5\}$. If $x \notin D_1$, then let $D = D_1 \cup \{u_2\}$. In both cases, the set $D$ is a 2DD-set of $G$, and so $\gamma_2^d(G) \le |D| \le |D_1| + 1 \le \floor*{\frac{2}{9}(n+k)}$, a contradiction.


