		Suppose that $V(T_v) = \{v_2,v_{8},v_{9}\}$ is an internal triangle of $G$. Let $G'$ be a graph of order $n'$ obtained from $G$ by deleting the vertices in $V_1^{7}\setminus\{v_2\}$, and let $G'$ have $k'$ vertices of degree~$2$. We note that $n' = n-6$ and $k' = k - 2$, and $v_2v_{8}$ is an outer edge of $G'$. Since $n \ge 13$, we have $n' \ge 7$. If $n'=7$, then by \cref{obs3}, there exists a 2DD-set $D'$ of $G'$ such that $|D'|= 2$ and $v_{2}\in D'$, and so $D'\cup \{v_1\}$ is a 2DD-set of $G$, and hence $\gamma_2^d(G)\le 3 \le  \floor*{\frac{2}{9}(n+k)}$, a contradiction. Hence, $n' \ge 8$. Let $G_1$ be a graph of order $n_1$ obtained from $G'$ by contracting the edge $v_2v_8$ to form a new vertex $x$ in $G_1$, and let $G_1$ have $k_1$ vertices of degree~$2$. By Lemma~\ref{key}, $G_1$ is a mop. Since $n' \ge 8$, we note that $n_1 = n' - 1 \ge 7$. By the minimality of the mop $G$, we have $\gamma_2^d(G_1) \le \floor*{\frac{2}{9}(n_1+k_1)} \le \floor*{\frac{2}{9}(n-7+k-2)} \le \floor*{\frac{2}{9}(n+k)}-2$. Let $D_1$ be a $\gamma_2^d$-set of $G_1$. If $x \in D_1$, then let $D = (D_1 \setminus \{x\}) \cup \{v_1,v_2,v_8\}$. If $x \notin D_1$, then let $D = D_1 \cup \{v_1,v_2\}$. In both cases $D$ is a 2DD-set of $G$, and so $\gamma_2^d(G) \le |D| \le |D_1| + 2 \le \floor*{\frac{2}{9}(n+k)}$, a contradiction.


