Clearly, every graph $\sU$ containing an $n$-vertex tree $T$ as an $(n-1)$-isometric subgraph, also contains $T$ as an isometric subgraph. Thus, by \cref{th:2trees}, $k^*(n) \le n-1$ for all $n\ge 1$. On the other hand, as we will see hereafter in \cref{th:k0}, there are pairs of $n$-vertex trees for which every minimum $1$-isometric-universal graph is not a tree whenever $n\ge 11$. Thus, $k^*(n) > 1$ for all $n\ge 11$.

