\par Before closing this section we would like to notice that there are certain piecewise-linear terms that are not captured by the model in \eqref{primal problem}. In particular, given a set of $K$ pairs $(C_r,d_r) \in \mathbb{R}^{l\times n} \times \mathbb{R}^l$ (with $K\geq 3$), where $r \in \{1,\ldots,K\}$, we could consider problems of the following form
\begin{equation*} 
\begin{split}
\underset{x \in \mathbb{R}^n}{\text{min}}&\quad \left\{f(x) + \sum_{i = 1}^l \max_{r \in \{1,\ldots,R\}} \{\left(C_{r} x + d_{r}\right)_i\}  + \delta_{\mathcal{K}}(x)\right\}, \\ 
\textnormal{s.t.} &\quad \ A x = b.
\end{split}
\end{equation*}
\noindent In this case, we could define the function $h$ as $h(u) \triangleq \sum_{i = 1}^l h_i(u_i)$, where $u_i \in \mathbb{R}^R$, and thus $u \in \mathbb{R}^{l R}$, with $h_i(u_i) \triangleq \max_{\tilde{i} \in \{1,\ldots,R\}} (u_i)_{\tilde{i}}.$ It is easy to prove that if $\Delta_R \triangleq \{w \geq 0\ \vert \ \sum_{i = 1}^R w_i = 1\}$ is the unit simplex on $\mathbb{R}^R$, then $h_i^* = \delta_{\Delta_R}$. Thus, in the subsequent SSN derivation, we would have to evaluate the Clarke subgradient of the projection onto the simplex. This was not considered in this work, and is left as a future research direction. This extension paves the way for the generalization of the proposed active-set scheme to a plethora of robust optimization problems that appear in practice (e.g. see \cite{OperRes:Berts}), greatly enhancing the generality of the proposed methodology. For example, in this case we could readily deliver an alternative to the recently proposed specialized interior point approach given in \cite{SIAMOpt:Gor} in the context of radiation therapy.
\par On the other hand, our assumption that $f$ (appearing in \eqref{primal problem}) is convex quadratic is utilized for simplicity. Indeed, the method readily extends to the case where $f$ is a general (twice continuously differentiable) nonlinear convex function. However, we should mention that, in this case, the theory developed in Section \ref{subsection: PMM convergence analysis} would have to be appropriately adjusted. Specifically, the proof concerning global convergence of the outer PMM scheme would still hold, under the same assumptions. However, since $f$ would be a general nonlinear function, the operator $T_{\ell}$ would no-longer be a polyhedral multifuction. Thus, Lemma \ref{lemma: error condition for polyhedral multifucntion} would no longer hold. Hence, in order to show local superlinear convergence of the proposed scheme (i.e., Theorem \ref{thm:local convergence}), we would need to additionally assume an appropriate metric subregularity condition (e.g., see the discussions in \cite{SIAMOpt:Lietal2,RockafellarGeneralizedPMM}).
