\begin{abstract}
% The rise of LLM-based Multi-Agent Planning (LMAP), particularly in Travel Planning, has leveraged advanced frameworks to enable autonomous and collaborative task execution. However, the reliance on vast online data introduces significant security risks, including vulnerabilities to fraudulent information. This study introduces a novel evaluation environment \textbf{WandaPlan} that integrates real-world data with embedded fraudulent content to assess the fraud-detection capabilities of MATP systems. Our experiments reveal that current agent frameworks, driven by metrics like task completion and user satisfaction, often fail to discern deceptive information, such as heavily discounted but unreliable travel deals. We identify critical vulnerabilities in these systems, demonstrating their susceptibility to online fraud. To address these challenges, we propose mitigation strategies to enhance the resilience and reliability of travel planning agents. Our findings highlight the urgent need to prioritize information security alongside utility in LLM-based multi-agent systems, ensuring trustworthy outcomes for users.

The rise of Large Language Model-based Multi-Agent Planning has leveraged advanced frameworks to enable autonomous and collaborative task execution. Some systems rely on platforms like review sites and social media, which are prone to fraudulent information, such as fake reviews or misleading descriptions. This reliance poses risks, potentially causing financial losses and harming user experiences. To evaluate the risk of planning systems in real-world applications, 
we introduce \textbf{WandaPlan}, an evaluation environment mirroring real-world data and injected with deceptive content. 
We assess system performance across three fraud cases: Misinformation Fraud, Team-Coordinated Multi-Person Fraud, and Level-Escalating Multi-Round Fraud. We reveal significant weaknesses in existing frameworks that prioritize task efficiency over data authenticity. At the same time, we validate WandaPlan's generalizability, capable of assessing the risks of real-world open-source planning frameworks. To mitigate the risk of fraud, we propose integrating an anti-fraud agent, providing a solution for reliable planning.

