\subsection{Elliptical particle moving in a viscous fluid: Jeffery's orbits}

Jeffery's orbits describe the periodic rotational motion of ellipsoidal particles suspended in a viscous fluid under shear flow conditions. The corresponding theory was developed for a creeping flow, i.e., for very low Reynolds numbers. In a cross-shear flow, non-spherical particles do not maintain a fixed orientation. Instead, their main axis undergoes continuous rotation that follows closed periodic trajectories on a sphere. The specific path of these orbits depends on the particle's initial orientation and its aspect ratio. In his paper \cite{Jeffrey_1922}, Jeffery derived an  analytical solution for the time-dependent orientation of the particle. In the two-dimensional case that we consider, the particle's main axis is confined to the shear plane. For an elliptical particle with the aspect ratio $r = a/b$, Jeffery’s analytical expressions for the time-dependent azimuthal angle $\theta$ and angular velocity $\dot\theta$ are given by
\begin{align*}
\theta(t) &= \tan^{-1}\left( \frac{b}{a} \tan\left( \frac{ab\,\dot{\gamma} t}{a^2 + b^2} \right) \right),\\
\dot{\theta}(t) &= -\frac{\dot{\gamma}}{a^2 + b^2}\Bigl(a^2\cos^2\theta + b^2\sin^2\theta\Bigr),
\end{align*}
where $\dot{\gamma}$ is the shear rate. A sketch of the computational
setup is presented in Fig. \ref{fig:jeffrey}. In Table \ref{tab:combined}, we summarize
the values of parameters used in our numerical experiments. Figure
\ref{fig:jeffrey_flow} shows a snapshot of the velocity magnitude
distribution for a
two-dimensional Jeffery orbit simulation.

