We simulated Jeffery's orbits using the Chimera-S and FBM approaches to check if the former method produces more accurate approximations to Jeffery's analytical solution
on the same background mesh. The FBM results shown in Fig. \ref{fig:jeffrey_fbm_vs_reference} were computed for two particle sizes (see Table~\ref{subtab:ellipse-sizes}) and two different refinement levels (see Table \ref{subtab:mesh-resolution}). In the case of small particles and low mesh resolution, there is a qualitative agreement with the reference solution, but we observe spurious oscillations and severe jitter of the Jeffery orbit curve. The inaccurate FBM[s,l1] approximation overshoots the minimum and undershoots the maximum significantly. Moreover, a phase shift can be observed. The numerical solutions labeled FBM[s,l2], FBM[l,l1] and FBM[l,l2] show the effect of improving the resolution by refining the mesh and/or increasing  the particle size. We see that higher resolution makes FBM approximations more accurate and diminishes numerical side effects (oscillations/jitter/phase errors). However, differences in the peak values are noticeable even on higher refinement levels.

The Jeffery orbits presented in Fig. \ref{fig:jeffrey_chimera_vs_reference}
were calculated using the
Chimera-S submesh method. They are much smoother than the FBM curves in Fig. \ref{fig:jeffrey_fbm_vs_reference}. Since the Chimera method is essentially body-fitted, it approximates Jeffery's analytical solution very well even on coarse resolution levels. To achieve comparable accuracy with FBM, one needs at least one extra global refinement, and the results are still much more sensitive to particle size. This is clearly reflected by the fact that for a small particle on level 1, the FBM error in the peak value is $\approx$ 9\%, whereas the corresponding Chimera-S error is $\approx$ 0.5\%. A global refinement of the background mesh brings the FBM error down to $\approx$ 3\%. In the case of a large particle, $\approx$ 3\% accuracy is achieved by FBM on level 1, and the error drops below 1\% on level~2. The corresponding Chimera-S error is less than 1\% already on the first level.


