\subsection{Experimental Setup}

We train our model on the CelebV-Dub~\cite{sung2025voicecraft} dataset, which is filtered from CelebV-HQ~\cite{zhu2022celebv} and CelebV-Text~\cite{yu2023celebv}. The training involves two stages: first, the Motion-DiT is trained from scratch using preprocessed keypoint representations from video frames, while the Audio-DiT is initialized from pretrained F5-TTS checkpoints. After these individual models converge, joint training is conducted on paired audio-visual data. All experiments were performed on 4 NVIDIA RTX 6000 Ada GPUs, with a single model variant (encompassing both training stages) taking approximately one day to train. Our training data features videos up to 30 seconds in length (with a much lower average), and we observed optimal performance when generating around 20 seconds of video. Consequently, for evaluation, longer videos are segmented into 20-second clips. 

For all evaluations, we consistently applied 32 Number of Function Evaluations (NFE), in line with F5-TTS, conducting tests on the CelebV-Dub test splits and the HDTF~\cite{zhang2021flow} dataset. Our quantitative assessment relied on established protocols: Word Error Rate (WER ↓) and reference audio similarity (SIM-o ↑) were measured following F5-TTS~\cite{f5tts2024}; Lip Sync Error confidence (LSE-C ↑) and distance (LSE-D ↓) were determined using Wav2Lip~\cite{prajwal2020wav2lip} methodologies; and speaker similarity (spkSIM ↑) was assessed according to VoiceCraft-Dub~\cite{sung2025voicecraft}. Additionally, Fréchet Inception Distance (FID ↓)~\cite{heusel2017gans} and Fréchet Video Distance (FVD ↓)~\cite{unterthiner2018towards} were computed using community standard implementations.

\subsection{Quantitative Evaluation}
In all tasks, our model benefits from an inpainting-based training paradigm, which enables flexible conditioning strategies. Depending on the task, we either generate both modalities jointly, provide clean inputs for one side while generating the other, or supply partial references as clues. This flexibility allows us to tailor the conditioning inputs to each task’s specific requirements—for example, using full expression keypoints in video-to-video generation, or fixed text and video for automated dubbing.

\paragraph{Talking head generation.}
We evaluate talking head performance on HDTF using four key metrics: FID, FVD, LSE-C, and LSE-D. As shown in Table~\ref{tab:talkinghead}, our model performs competitively or better with SOTA methods such as SadTalker, AniPortrait, and Hallo3. Notably, our method is primarily designed with a video-to-video (V2V) setup in mind, utilizing a sequence of 17 non-mouth expression keypoints as a `clue' (following~\cite{zhong2023identity}). This keypoint `clue' is used in both V2V and image-to-video (I2V) configurations: V2V warps frames sequentially from a source video, whereas I2V consistently warps an initial source image.

\paragraph{Text-to-speech generation.}
TTS performance is evaluated using WER and SIM-o on LibriSpeech-PC test-clean~\cite{f5tts2024, panayotov2015librispeech}. As shown in Table~\ref{tab:tts}, we observe a slight degradation in both WER and SIM-o scores, which we primarily attribute to the characteristics of our training dataset: CelebV-Dub. CelebV-Dub utilizes the Whisper ASR model~\cite{radford2023robust} to generate pseudo-captions and Spleeter~\cite{hennequin2020spleeter} to suppress background music. Unlike foundational TTS models trained on extensive ground truth (GT) data, our model's training on these pseudo-captions and demuxed audio likely contributes to this performance decrease. This hypothesis is supported by results from a variant of our model (Ours$^{\dagger}$) with a frozen Audio-DiT and no motion attention mask—a configuration similar to the original F5-TTS—which indeed achieves better TTS metrics. 
% As shown in Table~\ref{tab:tts}, our model achieves results on par with recent TTS models such as DiTTo-TTS and FireRedTTS. Importantly, due to the inpainting-based training design, our model remains robust under noisy or incomplete conditioning inputs, a setting where other models typically degrade.
% We note that our WER is slightly higher than these baselines. This can be attributed to the use of pseudo text generated by running Whisper on video data. In particular, we observed that Whisper occasionally hallucinates or appends incorrect final words, which negatively impacts the WER metric despite the overall quality and intelligibility of the generated speech remaining high.

\paragraph{Automated video dubbing.}
Thanks to the inpainting-based training paradigm, our model naturally extends to the automated video dubbing task. Given a video and target text, the model generates speech that aligns well with the speaker’s lip movements. 
Unlike the prior paragraph that performs lip-syncing by adapting pre-recorded speech to match a given video, automated video dubbing requires generating speech that is both semantically correct and temporally aligned with the speaker’s lip movements. In this setting, we observed that commonly used metrics such as LSE-C and LSE-D are insufficient for accurately evaluating the quality of generated audio. As discussed by~\cite{yaman2024audio, muaz2023sidgan, sung2025voicecraft}, SyncNet~\cite{chung2017out} is known to be sensitive to factors such as spatial translation (lacking shift invariance) and variations in audio/video codecs and compression. We specifically observed that SyncNet-derived metrics (LSE-C, LSE-D) can exhibit instability with codec alterations and, crucially, with the text-to-speech (TTS) output generated by our model. We refer readers to our supplementary materials for qualitative examples and further analysis. Despite the limitations of current metrics, our model consistently produces speech that matches the visual articulation, and achieves strong WER performance (Table~\ref{tab:dubbing}), supporting its effectiveness in this challenging task.

